# Card fraud decision engine

A fraud score is not a decision. This repository takes a LightGBM fraud score on
the IEEE-CIS card-not-present data and converts it into an approve / review /
decline policy chosen to maximise expected net benefit under a manual-review
capacity limit. It then measures, on a held-out time period, how much money and
customer disruption that policy saves compared with the cutoff an
AUC-oriented workflow would pick (Youden's J), a hand-written rules baseline, and
a model without velocity features.

The dollar figures depend on cost parameters that are **assumptions**, not
measurements. The claim the evidence supports is about the **ranking** of
policies across a pre-registered range of those assumptions, not about any
absolute dollar amount. See [Limitations](#limitations) directly below.

*This file is generated by `src/reporting/render.py` from `outputs/results.json`.
Every number below is read from that file; `make check` fails if one is not.*

## Limitations

Read these before the results. Full list with severity ratings: [FINDINGS.md](FINDINGS.md).

- **Cost parameters are invented.** Margin rate, churn penalty, review cost and
  analyst catch rate are defaults chosen in [PRE_REGISTRATION.md](PRE_REGISTRATION.md),
  not estimated from any portfolio. Absolute dollar results move by a large factor
  across the pre-registered grid (below); only the policy ranking is claimed.
- **Labels are chargebacks, not confirmed fraud.** `isFraud` marks reported
  chargebacks and propagates the label to later transactions on the same account.
  Some "fraud" is friendly fraud or disputes; some fraud is never charged back.
- **No true timestamp.** `TransactionDT` is a seconds offset from an undisclosed
  reference. It is used only to order rows and measure relative durations. Days
  and "per day" figures are in relative time.
- **Drift between training and test.** {{ M.n_significant | int }} of
  {{ M.n_features | int }} model features exceed a PSI of {{ M.psi_alert | num(2) }}
  between the training and test periods ({{ M.n_moderate | int }} more between
  {{ M.psi_warn | num(2) }} and {{ M.psi_alert | num(2) }}). Results are for one
  period of one merchant population.
- **One test period, one data source.** The test split covers
  {{ E.test_days | num(1) }} relative days and {{ E.test_rows | int }} transactions.
  Intervals below are bootstrap intervals over transactions within that period;
  they do not cover period-to-period variation.

